00:02:47.146 --> 00:02:48.817
<v Karimot Isiaka> My name is Karimot Isiaka.

00:02:48.872 --> 00:02:53.915
I am a product designer
with a strong interest in accessibility

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:55.547
and inclusive design.

00:02:56.891 --> 00:02:58.635
Here is the topic for the day.

00:02:59.038 --> 00:03:01.244
I will be sharing my
experience, basically,

00:03:01.726 --> 00:03:02.785
and the topic is:

00:03:02.809 --> 00:03:07.166
"What Accessibility Taught Me About
Building Better Content Experience."

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:08.727
Let's dive right in.

00:03:11.786 --> 00:03:14.073
So this is the agenda for the day.

00:03:14.686 --> 00:03:19.588
I would be taking you through why I chose
the topic and sharing my experience.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:26.604
I will talk about the research that led
to the information I'll be sharing today.

00:03:27.026 --> 00:03:33.480
I would also be talking about the pattern
behind the research findings, and also

00:03:34.401 --> 00:03:40.376
I'll be sliding in how we can
move from that research to practice

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.041
and how this can be applied to WordPress.

00:03:46.655 --> 00:03:47.856
So why this talk?

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:54.792
You know, they say that
accessibility gives access.

00:03:54.839 --> 00:04:00.178
So accessibility says give access
to everyone regardless of their ability.

00:04:00.685 --> 00:04:04.441
So this talk addresses how we
can make the access happen.

00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:08.097
So let's talk about where it began.

00:04:08.797 --> 00:04:14.742
It started when I got into the contribution
stage of the Outreachy internship,

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:22.731
and the task we were given
was to redesign a Wikipedia webpage,

00:04:22.832 --> 00:04:25.857
specifically the Wikipedia
community portal.

00:04:26.173 --> 00:04:29.784
So we were redesigning it
for accessibility requirement.

00:04:30.437 --> 00:04:33.580
And from designing it,

00:04:33.604 --> 00:04:38.883
what I learned
was that it was very important

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:42.200
that we pay attention
to the people that we are designing for.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:43.841
In this case,

00:04:44.272 --> 00:04:49.251
we were designing with different
accessibility considerations in mind,

00:04:49.693 --> 00:04:51.462
and that experience taught me.

00:04:52.099 --> 00:04:58.789
It moved me from designing a webpage
for accessibility requirement

00:04:59.248 --> 00:05:02.623
to realizing how much empathy,

00:05:03.218 --> 00:05:06.091
how much having
empathy for your visitors,

00:05:06.115 --> 00:05:08.389
like the visitors of your webpage,

00:05:08.413 --> 00:05:11.014
how much it shapes good design.

00:05:11.961 --> 00:05:18.109
So let's dive right in to get more
information about how that went.

00:05:19.998 --> 00:05:21.477
So the research stage.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.085
When I wanted to start
working on the project,

00:05:25.109 --> 00:05:27.853
of course, I didn't just
jump into designing.

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:31.293
I started with research.

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:36.934
First of all, there is the project
description, of course.

00:05:36.997 --> 00:05:38.509
So before we start,

00:05:39.428 --> 00:05:40.795
In the project description,

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:44.844
I already identified
what the problem we are trying to solve is.

00:05:45.514 --> 00:05:49.358
We are trying to redesign a webpage,

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:52.002
the Wikipedia Community Portal,
specifically.

00:05:52.674 --> 00:05:58.953
But we are designing it
to better fit the experience

00:05:58.977 --> 00:06:02.744
of people
who have different accessibility needs,

00:06:02.768 --> 00:06:04.171
whether they are visually impaired,

00:06:04.195 --> 00:06:07.847
whether they have cognitive disability,
motor impairment and the likes.

00:06:09.016 --> 00:06:11.658
I did some background digging.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.344
I read articles, I watched videos
on how to design for people

00:06:15.368 --> 00:06:18.370
with these various accessibility needs.

00:06:18.526 --> 00:06:23.567
I also had the…

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:25.538
I had access to some…

00:06:27.085 --> 00:06:31.420
I had access to looking
into the real accounts of people

00:06:31.768 --> 00:06:33.648
who have these experiences,

00:06:33.672 --> 00:06:40.215
I'll be sharing those different personas
with you as we go on in this talk.

00:06:41.873 --> 00:06:47.960
I looked at the example
of people who have visual impairment.

00:06:48.603 --> 00:06:50.824
There are different
personas that you would see.

00:06:50.848 --> 00:06:54.946
Visual--it covers visual impairment,
non-visual, motor

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.608
and cognitive disabilities.

00:06:56.865 --> 00:07:02.844
And we went a little bit deeper
with Stefan and Simone's experience,

00:07:02.875 --> 00:07:04.664
which you will get to see,

00:07:04.688 --> 00:07:09.139
because they have cognitive impairment
in their own case,

00:07:09.163 --> 00:07:14.793
which is not as popularly discussed
in the accessibility space

00:07:14.993 --> 00:07:17.275
compared to, like, the visual impairment.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:22.764
The first persona
we'll be looking at is Lexie,

00:07:22.788 --> 00:07:26.204
is an online shopper with colorblindness.

00:07:26.688 --> 00:07:30.173
So he cannot, in his case, Lexie cannot…

00:07:31.821 --> 00:07:38.289
In her case, Lexie cannot reliably
distinguish red, green, orange, brown.

00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:42.968
These colors that many sites
use alone to convey meaning.

00:07:43.944 --> 00:07:47.511
And she experiences some
barriers as a result of that.

00:07:47.539 --> 00:07:50.994
For instance, mandatory field,

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:53.941
all those mandatory field.

00:07:53.965 --> 00:07:55.327
Let's say you are…

00:07:56.501 --> 00:07:59.079
You missed a part…
When you're filling a form

00:07:59.394 --> 00:08:01.930
and there's a part that is mandatory,

00:08:02.651 --> 00:08:05.902
and the only way you can know
that it's mandatory

00:08:05.926 --> 00:08:10.182
is that they just convey the message
with like a color red,

00:08:10.206 --> 00:08:13.381
or they just used a color to outline--

00:08:15.017 --> 00:08:17.727
to communicate that requirement.

00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:24.021
So the barrier for her is that
these mandatory field errors marked in red

00:08:24.309 --> 00:08:28.159
are hard for her to spot
at checkout, for instance.

00:08:28.573 --> 00:08:31.859
So when she's checking out
and filling some forms,

00:08:32.337 --> 00:08:37.789
if it's just communicated via color,
she finds it very difficult.

00:08:39.428 --> 00:08:44.208
What works for her in this case
is that you don't rely on color alone.

00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:47.424
You pair it with icon,
labels, and patterns.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.610
Let's look at the next persona.

00:08:53.071 --> 00:08:57.108
This is Lakshmi, he's blind
and he uses screen reader.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.617
He's a senior accountant.

00:08:59.589 --> 00:09:03.658
He navigates a webpage
entirely through a screen reader.

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:10.943
Some of the barriers that he faces
is unlabeled images,

00:09:11.328 --> 00:09:15.473
unclear form fields,
and deeply nested navigation menus.

00:09:17.598 --> 00:09:19.439
These are the barriers that he faces.

00:09:20.327 --> 00:09:21.776
What works in his case?

00:09:22.673 --> 00:09:26.989
Proper heading structure
and labeled field forms.

00:09:27.622 --> 00:09:29.870
All of these things,
they are not just a nice-to-have.

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:32.714
We are going to look at more personas.

00:09:34.446 --> 00:09:36.377
In this case, here is Ade.

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:43.731
He's a reporter with limited use of his arm
following his spinal cord injury.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.305
So Ade relies mostly on keyboard.

00:09:47.402 --> 00:09:50.339
Sometimes a joystick
operated with his palm,

00:09:50.363 --> 00:09:54.558
and increasingly speech recognition.

00:09:56.282 --> 00:10:00.008
Basically, he switches method
depending on how tired he is.

00:10:00.797 --> 00:10:04.696
The barrier he faces is that
when he's tabbing through a page,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:10.333
he often can't tell
the element currently has focus

00:10:11.158 --> 00:10:16.355
since some sites lack
visible focus indicators.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:25.414
In his case, what works is when
pages have visual focus indicator,

00:10:25.438 --> 00:10:26.760
and here is an example.

00:10:30.237 --> 00:10:33.336
When you are designing a webpage,
for instance,

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.880
if your team is designing a webpage,

00:10:35.904 --> 00:10:39.257
you want to ensure that
for people like Ade,

00:10:41.058 --> 00:10:48.948
there is a visual focus indicator
that tells him that this is where he is

00:10:48.972 --> 00:10:50.839
at this point in the website.

00:10:51.191 --> 00:10:55.936
So let's say he is on a button.

00:10:57.728 --> 00:11:00.350
He should be able to know that,
yes, I'm on this button,

00:11:00.374 --> 00:11:03.561
as opposed to the button looking the same

00:11:03.585 --> 00:11:08.667
or staying the same
as it was before he put,

00:11:09.284 --> 00:11:13.438
he put his mouse across that button.

00:11:14.354 --> 00:11:16.929
This is what that looks like.

00:11:17.651 --> 00:11:20.779
This is just a button
showing you different states.

00:11:21.089 --> 00:11:22.697
From the default state to hover state,

00:11:22.721 --> 00:11:29.040
and it's showing you a different
color outline for the focus indicator.

00:11:32.983 --> 00:11:35.881
Let's go in deeper

00:11:35.905 --> 00:11:40.945
for personas who have
cognitive disabilities

00:11:40.969 --> 00:11:42.815
like dyslexia and ADHD.

00:11:42.839 --> 00:11:45.777
But before then, let's look
at the definition of both.

00:11:46.937 --> 00:11:50.930
According to the National Institute
of Neurological Disorders and Stroke,

00:11:50.954 --> 00:11:55.664
dyslexia is a brain-based
type of learning disability

00:11:56.515 --> 00:11:59.896
that specifically impairs
a person's ability to read.

00:12:00.883 --> 00:12:05.140
ADHD, on the other hand,
is a developmental disorder

00:12:05.164 --> 00:12:09.350
marked by persistent
symptoms of inattention,

00:12:09.374 --> 00:12:12.640
hyperactivity, and impulsivity.

00:12:13.463 --> 00:12:18.001
ADHD means
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder.

00:12:18.646 --> 00:12:23.046
I just wanted to set that
before we move into the personas

00:12:23.071 --> 00:12:26.306
who experience these disabilities.

00:12:26.962 --> 00:12:29.969
Stefan struggles.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.217
He has ADHD and dyslexia,

00:12:34.241 --> 00:12:39.809
so he has difficulty recognizing
and sounding out words,

00:12:40.168 --> 00:12:43.251
which affects his spelling
and reading comprehension.

00:12:44.393 --> 00:12:46.550
He also relies on text-to-speech

00:12:46.574 --> 00:12:51.096
so he can see and hear
words at the same time.

00:12:52.852 --> 00:12:56.434
This helps him focus,
especially on long text.

00:12:57.439 --> 00:13:02.292
It is very important to actually
have context of the type of people

00:13:02.308 --> 00:13:04.245
that may be visiting our website.

00:13:04.749 --> 00:13:08.144
If we have the context
of these different personas in mind,

00:13:08.491 --> 00:13:11.502
it helps to bring things into perspective,

00:13:11.526 --> 00:13:14.871
and it helps us also become
more intentional when we are designing.

00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:21.551
Stefan's… some of his struggle,
he gets lost in dense text with no breaks.

00:13:23.601 --> 00:13:29.129
He also get distracted by pop-ups,
moving ads and cluttered layouts.

00:13:29.419 --> 00:13:33.288
He struggles to navigate
sites with unclear structures.

00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:39.482
He misspells words while searching,
and he gets no results.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.113
Sites also don't respect
his browser settings.

00:13:44.878 --> 00:13:50.306
What works for him is
plain language, generous white space,

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.596
visual supports like icons,

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.903
simple, consistent layouts,

00:13:55.927 --> 00:14:01.607
sitemaps, breadcrumb trails
and search functionality.

00:14:02.181 --> 00:14:04.317
Another thing that works for him is

00:14:04.341 --> 00:14:09.055
design that adapts
to his user setting preference.

00:14:11.319 --> 00:14:14.228
Here is another persona.

00:14:14.394 --> 00:14:15.406
Here is Simone.

00:14:16.605 --> 00:14:20.764
Simone was diagnosed with dyslexia.

00:14:21.137 --> 00:14:25.949
In her case, she uses
specialist dyslexia software

00:14:25.973 --> 00:14:29.638
which reads out documents
and webpages for her,

00:14:29.662 --> 00:14:31.935
and it also helps her to read and spell.

00:14:32.765 --> 00:14:36.074
Even though the website…
I mean, the software helps her

00:14:36.098 --> 00:14:38.448
to feel more confident about her writing,

00:14:38.494 --> 00:14:43.131
she also asks her colleagues
to help her check important emails

00:14:43.155 --> 00:14:45.047
before sending it, just in case.

00:14:45.113 --> 00:14:49.367
So this is a use case for another persona.

00:14:50.462 --> 00:14:51.488
Some of her struggles:

00:14:51.498 --> 00:14:53.615
Forms take a long time to fill in,

00:14:54.302 --> 00:14:58.130
and she often needs help
checking her writing.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.760
Poor spelling, so she relies
on tools to guess what this meant.

00:15:03.145 --> 00:15:06.896
Loses concentration
around moving content banner and tickers.

00:15:08.228 --> 00:15:12.554
She struggles with dense writing
and complex language.

00:15:14.245 --> 00:15:18.893
Some of the things that work for her is
almost similar to the case of Stefan,

00:15:20.586 --> 00:15:22.549
one of the previous personas.

00:15:23.552 --> 00:15:25.677
The save and return functionality,

00:15:26.663 --> 00:15:27.996
reasonable timeouts.

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:35.214
This one is one
of the WCAG success criterion.

00:15:35.486 --> 00:15:41.098
We have to actually give
reasonable timeout for users

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.910
like Simone who have dyslexia.

00:15:45.266 --> 00:15:51.329
We want to… Let's say they are filling
a form and after 10 minutes of inactivity,

00:15:51.353 --> 00:15:53.535
the form would close.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.884
Instead of just closing the form directly,
you can…

00:15:57.712 --> 00:16:01.612
let's say two minutes
before the end of 10 minutes,

00:16:01.776 --> 00:16:05.201
you let them know
that the form will be closing

00:16:05.225 --> 00:16:10.320
so they can click the option
that allows them more time.

00:16:12.492 --> 00:16:17.374
They also want spell-tolerant
search and input tools

00:16:19.987 --> 00:16:24.009
that kind of allows them to,
let's say they are spelling…

00:16:24.033 --> 00:16:26.307
Maybe they didn't get the spelling wrong,

00:16:26.521 --> 00:16:31.180
you can build your site in such a way

00:16:31.204 --> 00:16:33.456
that recommends
the correct spelling for them.

00:16:33.987 --> 00:16:37.266
Avoid unnecessary motion on the page.

00:16:38.867 --> 00:16:44.347
Plain English,
like you don't have to be too complicated

00:16:44.965 --> 00:16:46.759
because for people like this,

00:16:46.783 --> 00:16:50.660
you want the content
on your website to be easy to understand.

00:16:51.185 --> 00:16:55.366
And adjustable contrast
between text and background.

00:16:55.679 --> 00:17:00.564
You can give them different option
to switch in-between light and dark mode.

00:17:00.809 --> 00:17:05.822
You can also add extra layers or something
that looks like what I have here,

00:17:05.846 --> 00:17:08.726
like a sepia background for your…

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.930
You can make that option
available for them.

00:17:12.113 --> 00:17:15.387
Also avoid justified text and italics.

00:17:16.604 --> 00:17:17.760
Let's move on.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.338
What the research revealed.

00:17:20.418 --> 00:17:24.822
Let's look at what all of…
Because studying all of these personas

00:17:24.846 --> 00:17:26.758
and learning about them,

00:17:27.774 --> 00:17:29.827
the research revealed a couple of things.

00:17:30.936 --> 00:17:33.402
Different people, different barrier,
one pattern.

00:17:33.445 --> 00:17:36.435
Let's look at what was…

00:17:36.459 --> 00:17:38.468
Even though they have
different experiences,

00:17:38.492 --> 00:17:41.482
let's look at the pattern
that continued to show up.

00:17:42.585 --> 00:17:46.340
Across every story, all of these people
that we've just read about,

00:17:46.581 --> 00:17:48.718
the specific barriers were different,

00:17:48.742 --> 00:17:51.296
but three things kept showing up
again and again,

00:17:51.851 --> 00:17:53.484
and they were the structure

00:17:55.838 --> 00:17:57.173
The things that make…

00:17:57.197 --> 00:18:01.595
these three things are what actually
make the page usable for them.

00:18:01.791 --> 00:18:04.626
The structure, clarity, and also control.

00:18:05.242 --> 00:18:07.892
Let's look at them
in a little more detail.

00:18:11.050 --> 00:18:13.456
What does these three things,
what do they look like?

00:18:13.572 --> 00:18:19.353
In the case of Lakshmi,
he navigates-- she navigates rather,

00:18:19.761 --> 00:18:21.291
she navigates by heading.

00:18:21.302 --> 00:18:25.572
So all of these structures,
they are not just a nice-to-have.

00:18:26.159 --> 00:18:32.395
For someone that uses screen readers,
she wants to be able to navigate well.

00:18:32.672 --> 00:18:41.088
And when your headings
are not properly cited and identified,

00:18:41.112 --> 00:18:46.481
if they are not properly structured,
for someone that uses a screen reader,

00:18:47.074 --> 00:18:49.892
she's going to come across
some challenges.

00:18:51.212 --> 00:18:52.609
She navigates by heading.

00:18:52.619 --> 00:18:57.339
You also want to ensure
that you are intentional

00:18:57.363 --> 00:19:02.052
about how you structure
the pages of your website.

00:19:02.638 --> 00:19:09.807
In Stefan's case, he needs plain language
and white space to stay consistent,

00:19:10.654 --> 00:19:13.208
to stay with the content
instead of giving up.

00:19:14.892 --> 00:19:19.850
Stefan needs plain language
and white space to stay with the content

00:19:19.874 --> 00:19:21.084
instead of giving up.

00:19:21.108 --> 00:19:27.846
In the next redesign
that I would be sharing with you,

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:29.568
you're going to see how this plays out.

00:19:30.464 --> 00:19:33.458
In Simone and Ade's case,
they need control.

00:19:33.764 --> 00:19:38.818
They want adjustable contrast, adjustable
timeout, to work on their own pace.

00:19:42.902 --> 00:19:45.824
All of these things are also supported by

00:19:45.848 --> 00:19:49.834
the British Dyslexia Association's
guideline.

00:19:51.324 --> 00:19:55.487
Everything that I just mentioned,
the plain language, the consistent structure,

00:19:55.725 --> 00:19:59.016
and also giving user control over content,

00:19:59.687 --> 00:20:01.695
over how content is presented.

00:20:02.014 --> 00:20:04.599
We are going to see more as we go further.

00:20:07.038 --> 00:20:10.024
From research to practice,

00:20:10.048 --> 00:20:14.693
how do we apply all of these things
to WordPress

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:17.639
when we are building websites on WordPress

00:20:18.019 --> 00:20:21.058
and when we are designing
content on WordPress?

00:20:25.612 --> 00:20:28.485
Everything we've covered,
the structure, the clarity, the control,

00:20:28.518 --> 00:20:30.512
they are not abstract ideas.

00:20:30.905 --> 00:20:33.996
They translate into
real, buildable decisions.

00:20:35.993 --> 00:20:37.812
What it looks like across…

00:20:39.174 --> 00:20:41.890
This is how it looks like
across four areas:

00:20:41.914 --> 00:20:46.570
content structure, navigation,
readability, and personalizations.

00:20:46.645 --> 00:20:48.567
Let's quickly look into that.

00:20:48.927 --> 00:20:52.302
For content structure,
it's not just decoration.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.195
It is how people move through pages,

00:20:55.532 --> 00:20:59.354
just like our previous user or persona

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:03.667
that is visually impaired
and works with a screen reader.

00:21:04.223 --> 00:21:08.779
Using real reading hierarchy
instead of just…

00:21:08.803 --> 00:21:10.407
Because sometimes when we,

00:21:11.661 --> 00:21:17.311
when we want to
Identify our headers,

00:21:17.691 --> 00:21:22.381
we sometimes make the mistake
of just making the header bold,

00:21:22.405 --> 00:21:27.789
but beyond that,
we need to actually name it.

00:21:28.371 --> 00:21:31.090
We need to style it as the heading.

00:21:31.317 --> 00:21:34.792
We need to style it as the heading,
as opposed to just making it bold.

00:21:36.644 --> 00:21:41.776
If it's just a written experience
where you're writing a letter

00:21:42.013 --> 00:21:47.766
and you're making the heading
part bolder, it works.

00:21:47.922 --> 00:21:55.247
But when it comes to the web,
you have to really define your header

00:21:55.294 --> 00:21:56.964
and structure it properly

00:21:56.988 --> 00:22:00.222
beyond just making it look like it

00:22:00.246 --> 00:22:07.422
but being able to structure it in a way
that the screen reader can catch it.

00:22:08.405 --> 00:22:10.612
Break content into short paragraphs.

00:22:11.023 --> 00:22:18.051
The British Dyslexia Association also
recommends roughly 100 characters per line.

00:22:18.106 --> 00:22:21.671
We would also see how that applied
in the redesign project that I worked on.

00:22:22.017 --> 00:22:26.866
Use lists and short sections
instead of dense blocks of text.

00:22:28.158 --> 00:22:29.606
So let's look at it.

00:22:30.153 --> 00:22:33.069
Before the redesign, the Wikipedia…

00:22:33.446 --> 00:22:37.743
Sorry, yes, the Wikipedia Community Portal
has been linked here.

00:22:37.743 --> 00:22:39.566
You can always check it out later.

00:22:41.578 --> 00:22:47.767
Here is some of the screenshots of the
original layout of the community portal.

00:22:53.425 --> 00:22:55.569
Yes. Let's keep going.

00:22:55.819 --> 00:22:58.962
Can we see how dense the contents look?

00:22:59.826 --> 00:23:03.558
And this, there is also
not enough white space,

00:23:03.582 --> 00:23:06.956
especially for users
like Stefan and Simone

00:23:07.445 --> 00:23:10.136
who need a lot of white space,

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:16.096
and they also do not learn well

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:21.754
when there's a lot of content
that are just very close together.

00:23:23.264 --> 00:23:24.956
Let's take a look at the redesign.

00:23:25.629 --> 00:23:31.545
In my case,
just learning about these users

00:23:31.569 --> 00:23:33.374
and understanding their challenges,

00:23:33.398 --> 00:23:40.214
I was able to empathize with them
and also redesign the community portal

00:23:40.238 --> 00:23:45.889
in such a way that allows
for what I've heard them say works for them.

00:23:47.177 --> 00:23:51.340
For instance, the
British Dyslexia Association

00:23:51.364 --> 00:23:56.515
already said that we should have
roughly 10 characters per line.

00:23:56.578 --> 00:24:02.290
I mean, I take that back,
100 characters per line on a webpage.

00:24:02.647 --> 00:24:06.868
So even if it will be more than 100,
it will not be too much.

00:24:06.994 --> 00:24:10.850
It will not be far off that it's too long

00:24:13.523 --> 00:24:17.552
that the user or the website visitor
is not able to follow along.

00:24:18.142 --> 00:24:19.925
This is what the redesign looks like.

00:24:20.288 --> 00:24:25.752
And if you look at this section
from the original website,

00:24:26.367 --> 00:24:29.131
it was not this spaced out.

00:24:29.207 --> 00:24:30.811
Everything was joined together.

00:24:31.851 --> 00:24:34.611
I was able to kind of categorize this

00:24:34.635 --> 00:24:40.814
and add some spacing to each of the boxes.

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:46.300
And also in the event part,

00:24:46.324 --> 00:24:48.404
Let's quickly go back for a bit.

00:24:48.485 --> 00:24:50.586
Look at this part that talks about event.

00:24:52.139 --> 00:24:56.404
The color contrast,
when I did the test, it was…

00:24:57.336 --> 00:25:01.873
it wasn't contrasting well because
there's, like, a light green background there,

00:25:01.931 --> 00:25:03.619
and there's a blue text.

00:25:03.704 --> 00:25:04.910
It was not contrasting well.

00:25:04.911 --> 00:25:07.778
But beyond that, there are two columns

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:11.371
for different things,

00:25:11.799 --> 00:25:13.151
and they are side by side.

00:25:13.398 --> 00:25:17.143
So this can… So they're
also very close together.

00:25:17.649 --> 00:25:19.070
They're a bit clumsy.

00:25:19.622 --> 00:25:22.177
And for someone who has dyslexia,
for instance,

00:25:22.201 --> 00:25:25.233
this is going to be difficult
for them to navigate.

00:25:27.254 --> 00:25:29.378
What I did was to…

00:25:29.604 --> 00:25:30.608
Yes.

00:25:30.632 --> 00:25:33.541
What I did in this part
was to make it easy

00:25:33.565 --> 00:25:38.309
because on the community portal page,
there is a lot of information.

00:25:38.325 --> 00:25:42.540
So I grouped this information
in such a way that…

00:25:42.564 --> 00:25:45.062
I used accordion to group the information

00:25:45.086 --> 00:25:50.890
so that when a visitor wants
to learn more details about,

00:25:50.914 --> 00:25:52.636
let's say, events and projects,

00:25:52.701 --> 00:25:55.701
they can click on it,

00:25:55.725 --> 00:26:00.486
and it brings out the details
about events and projects.

00:26:00.985 --> 00:26:06.378
If they want
about another section of the page,

00:26:06.402 --> 00:26:08.963
maybe the general notice, the signpost,

00:26:08.987 --> 00:26:13.027
they can always click on the accordion,

00:26:13.051 --> 00:26:15.446
and also it brings out the details,

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:18.494
as opposed to outlining
everything at once,

00:26:18.518 --> 00:26:22.100
and everything is in like a column,

00:26:22.124 --> 00:26:24.630
in a two-column section,

00:26:24.654 --> 00:26:29.152
and they're seeing two information
staring at them at the same time,

00:26:29.176 --> 00:26:30.610
and it's just so clumsy.

00:26:31.844 --> 00:26:38.811
So for another section, I also used
accordion to kind of space things out.

00:26:40.681 --> 00:26:46.429
For the footer
in the original community website,

00:26:46.453 --> 00:26:48.508
the content was…

00:26:49.192 --> 00:26:52.767
the content kind of started
from the beginning of the page

00:26:52.791 --> 00:26:54.158
to the end of the page,

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:58.338
and it was not… It was so long,

00:26:58.362 --> 00:26:59.903
and it would be easy for…

00:27:00.804 --> 00:27:04.176
It would also be easy for them to get lost

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:10.550
in the process of trying to fit
in all of the content at once.

00:27:15.538 --> 00:27:18.352
Let's go to the next part,
the navigation.

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:23.434
Navigation helps people
find their way, basically.

00:27:23.467 --> 00:27:28.575
So add a visible sitemap or content index.

00:27:28.931 --> 00:27:34.215
At the top of your page,
you can add a visible sitemap

00:27:34.239 --> 00:27:36.675
and content index that is clickable.

00:27:37.113 --> 00:27:39.870
So beyond it just being visible,
make it clickable.

00:27:40.322 --> 00:27:47.252
If they are like something
that looks like basically a content table

00:27:47.604 --> 00:27:55.436
where if a visitor wants
to navigate to a different section,

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:59.554
instead of scrolling all the way,
they can just click on it

00:27:59.578 --> 00:28:02.546
and it takes them right to this section.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:06.517
You can also,
to make the navigation easier,

00:28:06.541 --> 00:28:09.421
keep the navigation consistent
across pages.

00:28:09.689 --> 00:28:13.236
An example of this is the menus,

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:15.617
the way we label menu on our webpage.

00:28:15.664 --> 00:28:20.677
On the homepage, a lot of times
you will see the About,

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:24.041
you see maybe Blog,

00:28:24.515 --> 00:28:28.762
you would see Contact Us and the likes.

00:28:30.284 --> 00:28:32.355
The way it is arranged on one page

00:28:32.379 --> 00:28:35.898
is the way it should continue
to be arranged on all other pages.

00:28:36.271 --> 00:28:40.972
When you change that,
you can confuse your website visitors.

00:28:43.373 --> 00:28:44.535
Readability.

00:28:44.559 --> 00:28:49.145
This one, this particular one
cannot be overemphasized.

00:28:50.468 --> 00:28:56.750
Design is… You're not just designing
the interface to make it look good.

00:28:57.555 --> 00:29:00.903
The words, the text,
they are also part of the design.

00:29:01.171 --> 00:29:04.711
So they are not just…
You're not just designing the layout.

00:29:05.234 --> 00:29:09.753
your choice of fonts and the way
the text come up on your webpage,

00:29:09.811 --> 00:29:11.040
they are very, very important.

00:29:12.455 --> 00:29:15.478
Left-align the text.
Don't justify the text.

00:29:17.253 --> 00:29:24.083
This is also one of the recommendation
by the British Dyslexia Association.

00:29:24.657 --> 00:29:26.585
Avoid the use of italics

00:29:26.609 --> 00:29:32.584
because you know that, for instance,
someone with dyslexia,

00:29:33.238 --> 00:29:37.085
they find it difficult to read.

00:29:38.443 --> 00:29:42.464
So when the text also now don't
look like what they are used to,

00:29:43.087 --> 00:29:47.302
it can cause confusion for them.

00:29:47.824 --> 00:29:51.390
We want to make sure that
we are making things as clear as possible

00:29:51.818 --> 00:29:57.736
and not trying to make things difficult

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:00.885
because we are trying
to beautify our text.

00:30:01.496 --> 00:30:04.856
So use legible sans-serif for body text.

00:30:05.317 --> 00:30:08.696
Never rely on color alone
to convey meaning,

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:13.863
as we have seen from the experiences
of some of our personas.

00:30:14.333 --> 00:30:17.147
Avoid moving or auto-playing content.

00:30:18.061 --> 00:30:24.175
This can be distracting
for someone that experiences ADHD

00:30:24.199 --> 00:30:25.785
or that has ADHD.

00:30:26.407 --> 00:30:30.673
So in WordPress, avoid
animated hero sliders

00:30:30.697 --> 00:30:34.881
or auto-rotating carousels
on content-heavy pages.

00:30:35.768 --> 00:30:37.609
That can be distracting.

00:30:38.241 --> 00:30:40.352
It can be very, very distracting.

00:30:40.966 --> 00:30:42.437
This cannot be overstated.

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:48.094
If the user now decides
to play it themselves,

00:30:48.118 --> 00:30:53.218
give them an option to be able
to pause or play all these carousels.

00:30:56.402 --> 00:30:57.592
Personalization.

00:30:57.732 --> 00:31:04.738
Let users, let them, let your web page
visitors, let them shape the page.

00:31:04.833 --> 00:31:06.165
Let them not just view it.

00:31:06.903 --> 00:31:08.142
How can this happen?

00:31:08.497 --> 00:31:12.264
Let users adjust contrast,
font size and background.

00:31:13.731 --> 00:31:18.839
Respect their browser level accessibility
setting instead of overriding them.

00:31:19.137 --> 00:31:22.615
Design your website in such a way that

00:31:23.126 --> 00:31:31.854
it accommodates for the
browser setting of the visitor.

00:31:32.521 --> 00:31:37.745
Let it not override the setting
that they already have on their system

00:31:37.769 --> 00:31:40.470
or on their operating system
or on their browser.

00:31:40.697 --> 00:31:47.793
So in WordPress, you can use accessibility
plugin for reading mode or contrast toggle.

00:31:49.128 --> 00:31:54.452
In this next step says,
configure theme.json

00:31:54.476 --> 00:31:57.372
to respect user font size preference

00:31:57.703 --> 00:32:00.147
as a lower lift starting point.

00:32:00.295 --> 00:32:06.194
Basically, an example is
using flexible units like rem

00:32:06.311 --> 00:32:08.629
instead of fixed pixel values.

00:32:09.222 --> 00:32:14.786
So for your text size, use a flexible unit

00:32:15.691 --> 00:32:18.924
as opposed to using a fixed pixel value

00:32:18.948 --> 00:32:25.493
whereby the visitor, the website visitor

00:32:25.517 --> 00:32:31.713
is not able to change the text size
to fit into their own preference.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:35.313
So how do we…

00:32:35.354 --> 00:32:36.940
Let's go back to where we started from.

00:32:37.701 --> 00:32:45.763
You have learned about Lexie, Lakshmi,
Ade, Stefan, Simone,

00:32:45.787 --> 00:32:48.816
and you've seen a real redesign change.

00:32:54.071 --> 00:32:59.031
I was able to change all of this
when I understood what they needed.

00:32:59.539 --> 00:33:04.622
We basically moved from,
"This is the page I am designing,"

00:33:05.297 --> 00:33:09.298
to "How do I design for these people?"

00:33:09.322 --> 00:33:14.797
It was also my first time
of redesigning a page

00:33:14.821 --> 00:33:19.495
for people
that have diverse accessibility needs.

00:33:19.818 --> 00:33:27.156
It was my first time of considering
people who have cognitive disabilities.

00:33:28.272 --> 00:33:31.495
I was used to just
focusing on color contrast,

00:33:33.174 --> 00:33:36.013
also looking at…

00:33:36.037 --> 00:33:41.142
yes, majorly color contrast for people
with disability -- visual impairment.

00:33:41.427 --> 00:33:45.773
Also the heading labels,

00:33:46.227 --> 00:33:48.564
making sure
the heading is properly labeled.

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:51.216
But just being able to understand that

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:55.588
this is the way
someone who has cognitive disability,

00:33:55.612 --> 00:33:59.581
like ADHD and dyslexia, consume content,

00:33:59.605 --> 00:34:03.081
just being able to put myself--

00:34:03.105 --> 00:34:05.577
empathize with them,
put myself in their shoes

00:34:05.601 --> 00:34:10.739
and understand
how they consume content on webpages.

00:34:11.086 --> 00:34:14.973
I was able to understand
what their challenges are

00:34:14.997 --> 00:34:17.950
and think of the redesign

00:34:18.426 --> 00:34:21.357
from the angle where
I'm empathizing with them,

00:34:21.381 --> 00:34:28.109
and I'm also researching solutions
that fit into their own experience.

00:34:29.374 --> 00:34:35.813
I applied all of these solutions
that includes,

00:34:37.371 --> 00:34:39.446
using generous white space,

00:34:40.724 --> 00:34:43.673
not justifying my text,

00:34:44.529 --> 00:34:51.025
not using carousel anyhow on my webpage,

00:34:52.338 --> 00:34:55.156
and all of these things.

00:34:55.458 --> 00:34:58.433
And also not making sure,

00:34:58.457 --> 00:35:06.643
not using unnecessarily
long characters for my text.

00:35:07.574 --> 00:35:10.950
Number of characters on your--
on my text should not be too long.

00:35:10.963 --> 00:35:14.647
It should not be too long that it's--

00:35:17.086 --> 00:35:20.492
It's hard for someone
that has dyslexia to follow.

00:35:22.026 --> 00:35:26.436
All of this was
the things that I considered

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:29.104
when I was redesigning
the community portal,

00:35:29.128 --> 00:35:33.470
and I learned that compliance,

00:35:33.494 --> 00:35:36.448
my key takeaway,
compliance is not the finish line.

00:35:38.302 --> 00:35:41.892
A page can pass every technical check
and still be hard to use.

00:35:41.896 --> 00:35:50.321
For instance, you can run an automatic
accessibility check on a webpage,

00:35:50.345 --> 00:35:53.300
and it tells you,
"Oh, there's contrast issue here.

00:35:53.846 --> 00:35:57.119
There is… Alt text is missing here."

00:35:57.143 --> 00:36:00.297
But it does not really tell you…

00:36:00.321 --> 00:36:06.798
It maybe in a case
of the Wikipedia community portal page,

00:36:06.822 --> 00:36:11.188
it may not really tell you
that the content are clumsy

00:36:11.212 --> 00:36:13.699
and they are very packed together.

00:36:14.034 --> 00:36:20.531
And for someone like Stefan and Simone,
they would find it hard

00:36:20.946 --> 00:36:24.135
to actually read the content.

00:36:24.163 --> 00:36:30.679
They would find it hard to follow
the content structure of that type of page.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.673
So compliance is not the finish line.

00:36:34.134 --> 00:36:36.300
Even after the technical checks,

00:36:36.324 --> 00:36:42.918
you also want to consider
the experience of the people,

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:46.607
these type of personas
with different accessibility needs.

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:51.094
So structure, clarity, and control,
they matter as much as access.

00:36:52.204 --> 00:36:54.883
So these are the patterns
that show up again and again

00:36:54.907 --> 00:36:57.895
across all of this disability,

00:36:58.848 --> 00:37:01.318
across all of these
different disabilities.

00:37:01.844 --> 00:37:07.375
So another takeaway for me is:
small specific changes, they count.

00:37:07.575 --> 00:37:08.760
They really, really count.

00:37:09.052 --> 00:37:14.361
So heading hierarchy, plain language,
respecting user settings.

00:37:14.921 --> 00:37:20.526
None of these requirements
needs a full rebuild.

00:37:20.615 --> 00:37:24.150
None of all of this needs a full rebuild.

00:37:24.231 --> 00:37:29.835
It's just making small, small changes
from time to time,

00:37:29.859 --> 00:37:31.491
and before you know it,

00:37:31.515 --> 00:37:40.380
you are making the experience
of a user that might be experiencing

00:37:41.006 --> 00:37:44.877
or that might have a type of disability
that you're not aware of.

00:37:45.754 --> 00:37:49.837
You're making that
type of user's experience.

00:37:49.848 --> 00:37:55.100
You're improving it just by paying
attention to all of these tiny details.

00:37:56.916 --> 00:37:58.775
So three things to check this week.

00:38:00.933 --> 00:38:02.321
Check your heading structure.

00:38:02.752 --> 00:38:06.682
Are you using real H2s, H3s and the likes?

00:38:07.497 --> 00:38:11.961
All these different heading types,
are you using them

00:38:11.985 --> 00:38:18.370
or bold text styled
to look like the real header?

00:38:19.403 --> 00:38:26.408
Check if that's what you're doing
on your webpage or you're using bold text.

00:38:27.057 --> 00:38:33.382
Does it just look like it
or it is actually a real header

00:38:33.406 --> 00:38:36.355
or it has a real heading structure?

00:38:37.286 --> 00:38:43.877
Turn off auto-rotating carousels
or moving content on content-heavy pages.

00:38:44.962 --> 00:38:51.674
Again, we found out that this
can be really distracting for different users

00:38:52.524 --> 00:38:57.145
that have dyslexia or ADHD,

00:38:57.169 --> 00:38:58.648
but they are not the only ones.

00:38:58.686 --> 00:39:06.495
Even users who have autism,
they can find this very triggering.

00:39:07.027 --> 00:39:12.211
So don't auto-rotate carousel content,

00:39:12.235 --> 00:39:15.055
videos, images, sliding up and down.

00:39:15.075 --> 00:39:18.894
It can be very… It can make
the user experience very difficult.

00:39:19.183 --> 00:39:23.009
So you want to pay attention
when you are designing your web content.

00:39:23.721 --> 00:39:27.096
Zoom your site to 300 percent
and see what breaks

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.025
so that you can start fixing it.
Okay?

00:39:30.498 --> 00:39:35.167
These are important links
that you also want to check out when…

00:39:35.663 --> 00:39:38.044
Just to learn more

00:39:38.068 --> 00:39:45.075
as you build better
web content experiences

00:39:45.099 --> 00:39:49.502
for the users that visit your webpage.

00:39:49.734 --> 00:39:54.332
And also, as you continue
to build websites on WordPress,

00:39:54.902 --> 00:39:56.347
you want to learn more

00:39:56.371 --> 00:40:01.765
about how you can make
the web experience of your users better.

00:40:03.690 --> 00:40:05.267
Oh, thank you for joining me.

00:40:05.321 --> 00:40:07.377
I hope you had a great time.

00:40:14.254 --> 00:40:15.793
<v Gary> Thanks very much, Karimot.

00:40:15.817 --> 00:40:17.495
Yeah, that was wonderful.

00:40:19.253 --> 00:40:21.114
Joining you live from Nottingham,

00:40:21.138 --> 00:40:25.779
Robin Hood, he can't make it today
because his internet connection is bad,

00:40:25.844 --> 00:40:31.137
but it's really nice to join you,
and thanks very much

00:40:31.161 --> 00:40:32.464
for the going through that.

00:40:34.238 --> 00:40:39.495
It's really good to see not just
you're talking about,

00:40:39.519 --> 00:40:43.297
obviously we need to cater
for people with visual disabilities,

00:40:43.321 --> 00:40:47.663
but also talking about
people with motor disabilities.

00:40:48.131 --> 00:40:50.447
And I have to say, I feel seen.

00:40:50.448 --> 00:40:52.691
I've got autism. I've got ADHD.

00:40:53.136 --> 00:40:57.853
I haven't got dyslexia, but I've got
something similar called Irlen syndrome,

00:40:57.853 --> 00:41:01.948
which means I have trouble reading,
and I wear these overlays on my glasses.

00:41:01.948 --> 00:41:08.328
And so just to say the difference between
the before and after with your redesign,

00:41:09.459 --> 00:41:13.481
it's so much more
relaxing to see that website.

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.049
It looks so much better.

00:41:15.073 --> 00:41:17.538
So, yeah, well done on that.

00:41:18.298 --> 00:41:21.140
<v Karimot> Thank you, Gary.
<v Gary> someone was asking… Oh, you're welcome.

00:41:21.164 --> 00:41:24.558
Someone was asking,
is the redesign now live of that page?

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:27.793
<v Karimot> Is the what?

00:41:28.272 --> 00:41:31.575
<v Gary> Is the redesigned page,
is that live now?

00:41:33.277 --> 00:41:34.529
<v Karimot> No, it's not live.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:36.370
No, it's not live.

00:41:36.394 --> 00:41:40.263
But I think that it's a big organization

00:41:40.287 --> 00:41:44.053
and their processes can be…

00:41:45.686 --> 00:41:49.078
Their design decisions--because
it was an internship that I did,

00:41:49.677 --> 00:41:53.085
A couple of changes that I recommended…

00:41:53.633 --> 00:41:57.438
Okay, I think one change that I recommended
that I didn't mention in the video

00:41:57.462 --> 00:41:59.797
was the placement of…

00:42:00.433 --> 00:42:02.779
So for users that are logged out,

00:42:02.803 --> 00:42:09.953
there is a particular icon
that was initially in-between

00:42:09.977 --> 00:42:16.648
the login and I think the--
there are, like, two primary buttons.

00:42:17.005 --> 00:42:20.651
So that particular icon
was in between them.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:26.503
So I recommended during my redesign
that it be moved to the side,

00:42:26.527 --> 00:42:28.680
because they don't belong
to the same category.

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:32.133
And by the time I actually
resumed the internship,

00:42:32.157 --> 00:42:34.259
I saw that it has been moved.

00:42:35.875 --> 00:42:39.653
Even though this is, like,
a very simple detail,

00:42:39.677 --> 00:42:43.338
it can go a long way
in improving the user experience.

00:42:43.456 --> 00:42:48.560
So when it comes to accessibility
and just ensuring that

00:42:49.131 --> 00:42:53.944
the different people
with accessibility needs are considered,

00:42:53.968 --> 00:42:55.111
The little things matter.

00:42:55.135 --> 00:42:58.522
You don't have to
change everything at once.

00:42:58.564 --> 00:43:00.423
You can do it a step at a time.

00:43:00.781 --> 00:43:02.441
It's an ongoing process.

00:43:02.623 --> 00:43:03.680
Yeah.

00:43:04.278 --> 00:43:09.485
<v Gary> Absolutely. Well, I hope that they
follow through with your other recommendations,

00:43:09.509 --> 00:43:12.179
'cause it makes a big difference there.

00:43:13.602 --> 00:43:16.495
I've just,
I've got loads of questions here.

00:43:16.815 --> 00:43:20.729
Just one really good question is that,

00:43:20.753 --> 00:43:26.209
yeah, we've talked about some users
are struggling with pop-ups and moving items.

00:43:26.799 --> 00:43:31.346
But marketing teams are always
really keen on that because it,

00:43:31.834 --> 00:43:34.387
to drive engagement and conversions.

00:43:34.864 --> 00:43:42.417
I'm just wondering how we can advocate
for clarity and white space

00:43:42.441 --> 00:43:46.047
when the businesses are insisting on

00:43:46.221 --> 00:43:51.260
let's be intrusive, let's distract people
with this advert, this pop-up,

00:43:52.343 --> 00:43:54.073
'cause they want to
hit those revenue goals.

00:43:54.089 --> 00:43:58.973
How can we persuade them otherwise?

00:44:00.189 --> 00:44:04.779
<v Karimot> Okay. I think that you come with facts,

00:44:04.803 --> 00:44:09.246
especially whoever is proposing that,
you come with facts.

00:44:09.281 --> 00:44:11.684
You can conduct some user research

00:44:11.708 --> 00:44:17.706
with the people who are being affected
by this type of design style.

00:44:18.925 --> 00:44:22.992
because whether you like it or not,
there are thousands of--

00:44:23.016 --> 00:44:25.381
hundreds of thousands
of people across the world

00:44:25.405 --> 00:44:30.872
who experience different forms
of impairment or disability,

00:44:31.281 --> 00:44:34.721
and you're counting them out when you…

00:44:34.745 --> 00:44:36.128
It also affects your business.

00:44:36.139 --> 00:44:42.118
you can come
from the angle of a business case.

00:44:43.933 --> 00:44:47.990
you also have to say
that whatever you're proposing,

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:52.559
they still get to see it,
but they just see it in a different way.

00:44:54.136 --> 00:44:58.905
even though I've not been
diagnosed with dyslexia or ADHD,

00:44:59.192 --> 00:45:03.517
when I visit a website
and everything is moving at the same time,

00:45:03.541 --> 00:45:07.777
I start feeling very uncomfortable,
and I leave.

00:45:07.826 --> 00:45:11.849
I don't get to complete the task
that I went there for in the first place.

00:45:12.128 --> 00:45:14.400
So if I was supposed to…

00:45:16.961 --> 00:45:19.749
if I'm supposed to be converted,

00:45:21.846 --> 00:45:25.227
maybe if that was not the case,
I would have been one of their,

00:45:25.251 --> 00:45:27.544
maybe I would have
signed up to their mailing list

00:45:27.568 --> 00:45:33.245
or been able to consume whatever it is
that they wanted to market to me.

00:45:33.494 --> 00:45:37.447
But because I have already left
the webpage, they've already…

00:45:37.471 --> 00:45:41.713
I'm one of many people that they've lost.

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:47.454
So you need to, whoever is proposing that
to their, to their marketing team,

00:45:47.478 --> 00:45:48.751
you need to come with facts.

00:45:48.781 --> 00:45:50.719
You need to do your research

00:45:50.743 --> 00:45:55.991
so that you're not just
talking based on what you feel.

00:45:56.342 --> 00:45:59.357
So come with facts and your numbers

00:45:59.381 --> 00:46:03.501
so that they can see that there's also a
business case from what you're proposing.

00:46:04.918 --> 00:46:08.039
<v Gary> Yeah. The previous talk
talked about business cases

00:46:08.063 --> 00:46:14.226
and the amazing amount of return on investment
that accessibility can have.

00:46:14.233 --> 00:46:15.938
So yeah, that's really good to hear.

00:46:17.108 --> 00:46:21.480
Someone's asking as well,
do you have go-to sources?

00:46:21.483 --> 00:46:25.671
Do you have existing sources
for your accessibility personas,

00:46:25.695 --> 00:46:27.646
or do you think
it's better to build your own?

00:46:29.477 --> 00:46:30.828
<v Karimot> Okay, yes, there are some.

00:46:31.644 --> 00:46:32.651
Okay.

00:46:33.289 --> 00:46:39.953
The personas that I actually
highlighted during my talk,

00:46:39.977 --> 00:46:42.531
they are actually on…

00:46:42.555 --> 00:46:47.120
You can find some of them on the gov.uk.

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:49.292
And if you have…

00:46:49.316 --> 00:46:50.961
Okay, I think the slides are here.

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:53.003
They've shared the slides
of my talk already.

00:46:53.824 --> 00:47:00.951
You can actually read the interviews
and real-life experiences of these personas.

00:47:01.272 --> 00:47:05.109
So instead of building yours,
you can use them as references.

00:47:05.447 --> 00:47:09.921
And you can also use Google to just search

00:47:09.945 --> 00:47:15.929
the experiences of people who have talked
about their own personal life experience

00:47:15.953 --> 00:47:18.320
on how they navigate the web

00:47:18.693 --> 00:47:21.664
and just digital interfaces in general.

00:47:21.843 --> 00:47:23.199
So you don't have to build yours,

00:47:23.223 --> 00:47:25.781
but if there are people that you know,

00:47:25.805 --> 00:47:30.612
you can, get in contact with them

00:47:30.636 --> 00:47:32.486
and ask that you learn about--

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:34.856
that you're interested
in learning about their experiences.

00:47:34.865 --> 00:47:38.174
But in my case, the personas
I cited during my talk,

00:47:38.198 --> 00:47:39.892
they are actually…

00:47:39.916 --> 00:47:41.444
I didn't know them in person,

00:47:41.468 --> 00:47:44.604
but they were interviewed
by these platforms

00:47:44.628 --> 00:47:45.989
and they were…

00:47:46.649 --> 00:47:48.958
It allowed me to actually
empathize with them.

00:47:49.095 --> 00:47:51.698
One of them, I was able to watch his video

00:47:51.722 --> 00:47:54.049
where he actually
shared his own experience,

00:47:54.073 --> 00:47:58.516
so I heard from his…
It was like a firsthand experience

00:47:58.518 --> 00:48:01.253
just watching him talk
about his experience,

00:48:01.277 --> 00:48:05.514
how he reads through the web,
and how he consumes information.

00:48:05.746 --> 00:48:06.794
Yeah.

00:48:07.902 --> 00:48:12.184
<v Gary> That's excellent.
There's so much material out there.

00:48:12.212 --> 00:48:15.800
Without having to come up
with your own personas,

00:48:15.824 --> 00:48:18.511
you can still find
lots of information out there

00:48:18.535 --> 00:48:23.573
and as you say, come up with some empathy

00:48:23.597 --> 00:48:29.923
and some ideas about
all the different challenges people face.

00:48:29.947 --> 00:48:31.452
So yeah, that's brilliant.

00:48:31.511 --> 00:48:33.923
Well, thank you very much, Karimot.

00:48:34.066 --> 00:48:38.881
We've run out of time
but thanks very much for your talk today.

00:48:39.814 --> 00:48:44.533
Thank you for attending
this session with Karimot Isiaka.

00:48:45.149 --> 00:48:49.055
You can continue the conversation
in Slack or on social media

00:48:49.059 --> 00:48:56.893
using #WPA11yDay and #WPAD2026.